<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Ottobeuren</h1><p class="st-meta">1299 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Ottobeuren</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Benedictine abbey in the Swabian Allgäu, founded in 764, whose abbots ruled a small territory and built one of Germany's grandest Baroque churches.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Founded in 764 by the Swabian noble Silach and protected by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, the abbey built up lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunz">Günz</a> valley. From the 13th century the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-augsburg">Augsburg</a> held its advocacy, and the abbots spent centuries asserting their independence; only in 1710 did the abbey buy out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg's</a> rights and secure full imperial immediacy.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The abbot ruled several villages as a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-circle">Swabian Circle</a> and of the prelates' bench of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>. Income from farms, forests and tithes paid for learning and building: from 1711 Abbot Rupert Ness rebuilt the monastery, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anselm-erb">Anselm Erb</a> the great church was completed to designs by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-michael-fischer">Johann Michael Fischer</a>, a masterpiece of south German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The abbey was secularised in 1802 and its territory annexed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-bavaria">Electorate of Bavaria</a>. Monks returned in 1834, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottobeuren">Ottobeuren</a> is again a Benedictine community whose basilica and organs draw many visitors.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-ottobeuren.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Ottobeuren" width="320" height="296"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bas%C3%ADlica,_Ottobeuren,_Alemania,_2019-06-21,_DD_103.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1299 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ottobeuren</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rupert Ness, Anselm Erb</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ottobeuren Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1550&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-ottobeuren" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Ottobeuren on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4920 499 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Ottobeuren lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5240 755L5229 694M5240 755L5269 749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5240 755h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5240 755h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1550&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-ottobeuren">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
